1. Outside view. What is the base rate here? What has this series done historically, how much does it move between releases, and what would a naive extrapolation predict?
Historically, iPhone holds the dominant share in this five-brand basket, sitting comfortably around 39-41% during the quiet summer months. In mid-September, when Apple's annual hardware event occurs, iPhone’s share typically spikes drastically (up to ~54% as seen for the week of Sept 12) and then steadily decays. Tesla typically oscillates between 8.5-10.5%. Samsung rests around 12-14%. Netflix and Disney share similar levels of baseline interest (roughly 17-21%), absorbing the leftover market share based inversely on how much oxygen Apple takes up. A naive extrapolation of the most recent week (Sept 26) would yield an iPhone share continuing its normalization slope down from 45.8% to about 42-43%, with the other four brands rising proportionally.

2. Inside view. What is specific to this release -- events, timing, anything that would move this particular number away from the base rate? Say how much each is worth, in the unit of the series.
The defining event of the forecast period is the out-of-cycle announcement of the "iPhone Duo," Apple's foldable phone, on Tuesday, Sept 29. Because the announcement fell during the "missing" week (Sept 27 - Oct 3), that week likely absorbed the heaviest spike in search interest (likely pushing iPhone back over 50%). For the target week (Oct 4 - Oct 10), the initial announcement hype will have cooled down. However, the device "doesn't hit stores till next month" (October), meaning pre-orders or store releases might occur toward the end of our target week. This will keep iPhone searches elevated but not at absolute peak levels. I expect iPhone to capture ~43.5%, holding onto +3% above its summer base rate but dropping -2.3% from the Sept 26 reading.
For the remaining brands, the leftover 56.5% will distribute mostly according to recent post-summer baselines. Tesla has a "Texas launch event" brewing for its Roadster, which could slightly bump its share (+0.3%) toward 9.5%. Samsung is making corporate AI investments but lacks a consumer hardware splash, keeping it subdued around 11.8%. Netflix and Disney are entering the early Halloween window, generally a strong engagement period for streaming, raising them to roughly 17.0% and 18.2% respectively.

3. Pre-mortem. Assume your answer turns out badly wrong. Write the most likely reason, then correct for it.
If the forecast is badly wrong, it is likely because the iPhone Duo release date lands squarely on Friday, Oct 9, causing a massive secondary launch-day search spike that rivals the initial announcement. In that scenario, iPhone could surge back above 50%, compressing all other brands heavily. Conversely, if no consumer action happens for the Duo until late October, iPhone could plunge straight back to its 39-40% baseline. I will use a moderately wide standard deviation for iPhone (3.5 points) and corresponding proportional widths for the other brands to cover these distinct release-timing scenarios. 

4. Calibrate. Your sd should be wide enough that the true value falls inside one sd about two thirds of the time. Check it against how much this series actually moves between releases.
Historically, iPhone can shift by 5 to 10 points on an event week, but typically shifts by 2 to 4 points during the decay phase. A 3.5 SD for iPhone correctly sizes a ~68% confidence interval of 40% to 47%. The smaller brands usually shift by 0.5 to 2 points week-to-week; giving them SDs between 1.0 and 1.6 scales perfectly with their respective shares of the residual variance.

